"The occupation time of a random walk generated by the uniform random permutation--a GPT-6 Astra proof (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.25102)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: The occupation time of a random walk generated by the uniform random permutation--a GPT-6 Astra proof ... This note provide a proof of Conjecture 2.5 in Fang et al. ... We show that the number of edges lying above zero of a random walk generated from the uniform permutation has the discrete arcsine law.\nTitle: The occupation time of a random walk generated by the uniform random permutation--a GPT-6 Astra proof\nAuthors: Wenpin Tang\nDate: Sun Sep 20 01:45:00 2026\n\nThis note provide a proof of Conjecture 2.5 in Fang et al. (J. Appl. Probab., 58(4):851--867, 2021), with the help of \\texttt{GPT-6 Astra}. We show that the number of edges lying above zero of a random walk generated from the uniform permutation has the discrete arcsine law. The proof is short but hinges on an unexpected representation. We also make several comments on solving the problem using large language models.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJ. Appl. Prob. 58, 851–867 (2021) (https://www.columbia.edu/~wt2319/Arcsine.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.0 years ago; Crawled: 4.9 years ago; 58, 851–867 (2021) ... We also give an unexpected conjecture, along with numerical evidence and a partial proof in special cases, for the result that the number of steps above the origin by step 2n for the uniform permutation generated walk has exactly the same discrete arcsine distribution as for the simple random walk, even though the other statistics for these walks have very different laws. ... ****** Postal address: Department of Statistics and Applied Probability, National University of Singapore, 6 Science Drive 2, Singapore 117546.\nJ. Appl. Prob. 58, 851–867 (2021)  \ndoi:10.1017/jpr.2021.14\n\nARCSINE LAWS FOR RANDOM WALKS GENERATED FROM RANDOM PERMUTATIONS WITH APPLICATIONS TO GENOMICS\n\nXIAO FANG,* The Chinese University of Hong Kong  \nHAN L. GAN,** Northwestern University  \nSUSAN HOLMES,*** Stanford University  \nHAIYAN HUANG,**** University of California, Berkeley  \nEROL PEKÖZ,***** Boston University  \nADRIAN RÖLLIN,****** National University of Singapore  \nWENPIN TANG,******* Columbia University\n\nAbstract\n\nA classical result for the simple symmetric random walk with 2n steps is that the number of steps above the origin, the time of the last visit to the origin, and the time of the maximum height all have exactly the same distribution and converge when scaled to the arcsine law. Motivated by applications in genomics, we study the distributions of these statistics for the non-Markovian random walk generated from the ascents and descents of a uniform random permutation and a Mallows(q) permutation and show that they have the same asymptotic distributions as for the simple random walk. We also give an unexpected conjecture, along with numerical evidence and a partial proof in special cases, for the result that the number of steps above the origin by step 2n for the uniform permutation generated walk has exactly the same discrete arcsine distribution as for the simple random walk, even though the other statistics for these walks have very different laws. We also give explicit error bounds to the limit theorems using Stein’s method for the arcsine distribution, as well as functional central limit theorems and a strong embedding of the Mallows(q) permutation which is of independent interest.\n\nKeywords: Brownian motion; Lévy statistics; Mallows permutation; Stein’s method; strong embedding; uniform permutation\n\n2010 Mathematics Subject Classification: Primary 60C05  \nSecondary 60J65; 05A05\n\nReceived 4 February 2020; revision received 15 February 2021.  \n* Postal address: Department of Statistics, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Shatin, N.T., Hong Kong. Email: xfang@sta.cuhk.edu.hk  \n** Postal address: University of Waikato, Private Bag 3105, Hamilton 3240, New Zealand. Email: han.gan@waikato.ac.nz  \n*** Postal address: Department of Statistics, 390 Jane Stanford Way, Stanford University, Stanford, CA 94305-4020. Email: susan@stat.stanford.edu  \n**** Postal address: Department of Statistics, University of California, Berkeley, 367 Evans Hall, Berkeley, CA 94720-3860. Email: hhuang@stat.berkeley.edu  \n***** Postal address: Boston University, Questrom School of Business, Rafik B. Hariri Building, 595 Commonwealth Avenue, Boston, MA 02215. Email: pekoz@bu.edu  \n****** Postal address: Department of Statistics and Applied Probability, National University of Singapore, 6 Science Drive 2, Singapore 117546. Email: adrian.roellin@nus.edu.sg  \n******* Postal address: Department of Industrial Engineering and Operations Research, Columbia University, 500 W. 120th Street #315, New York, NY 10027. Email: wt2319@columbia.edu\n\n© The Author(s), 2021. Published by Cambridge University Press on behalf of Applied Probability Trust.\n\n851--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKolKom 2026 — 43rd Kolloquium über Kombinatorik (https://kolkom26.zib.de/)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Star Starred ... “A proof of the Ryser-Brualdi-Stein conjecture for large even $n$.” arXiv preprint (2023), arXiv:2310.19779. ... Discussiones Mathematicae Graph Theory 41(2), pp. ... Introduced in the 1970s by Nešetřil, Pultr, and Rödl [6], [7] – and motivated by the work of Dushnik and Miller, as well as by the induced Ramsey theorem – determining the Prague dimension of a graph is a notoriously hard problem. ... Recently, Kardoš, Máčajová and Zerafa [6] proved that given an arbitrary set of pairwise edge-disjoint odd cycles, say $\\mathcal O$, in a bridgeless cubic graph, there exists a $1$-factor intersecting all cycles in $\\mathcal O$ in at least one edge.\n## Sergiy Kozerenko\n\nKyiv School of Economics\n\n### Triameter of graphs: recent progress and open problems\n\nThe triameter of a connected graph $G$ is the largest value of $d(u,v)+d(u,w)+d(v,w)$ over all triples of vertices of $G$. The name and the sharp bound $\\operatorname{tr}(G)\\le 2n-2$ for graphs with $n$ vertices go back to the work of Laurent [12] on the metric polytope in the 1990s. Later, this parameter arose independently in lower bounds for radio $k$-colorings and in bounds on the total domination number, and its systematic study started around 2020. We survey the development of the topic. In 2021, Das [1] posed four questions on the triameter: three of them were answered in 2022, and the remaining question was answered in 2026. Three further problems on median and distance-hereditary graphs, posed in 2022 [5], have recently been addressed as well, partly on MathOverflow [13] and partly in a preprint with formally verified proofs [3]. We also discuss graphs with small triameters, and announce new results obtained jointly with A. Hak: we bound the triameter in terms of distance packings, which confirms a conjecture of Das and Abdioğlu [2] that $\\operatorname{tr}(G)\\le 6n/(\\delta(G)+1)$, and determine the maximum triameter of graphs with given order and vertex connectivity. We conclude with a list of open problems.\n\ngraph parametersstructural graph theory\n\n#### References\n\n  1. A. Das. “Triameter of graphs.” Discussiones Mathematicae Graph Theory 41(2), pp. 601–616 (2021), DOI:10.7151/dmgt.2212, arXiv:1804.01088.\n  2. A. Das and C. Abdioğlu, The triameter of a graph, Necmettin Erbakan Univ. J. Sci. Eng. 7(3) (2025), 535–543., DOI: 10.47112/neufmbd.2026.112\n  3. H. Dong, Answers to three problems of Hak, Kozerenko and Oliynyk on the triameter of graphs, preprint, 2026. https://github.com/Horace-Maxwell/ai4math-results.\n  4. A. Hak and S. Kozerenko, Triameter, distance packings, and vertex connectivity, in preparation, 2026.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPeter Winkler - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/peter-winkler)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; 2026-09-21 v2 ... That the answer (namely, $4^{-n}\\binom{2n}{n}$) is independent of $μ$ follows from a classical theorem of Darling, stating that for symmetric atomless increments, the distribution of each individual rank in the permutation obtained by ranking the partial sums is independent of the step law. ... We give a self-contained proof and extend the result to the permutations generated by partial sums of uniformly random signed permutations of any fixed, finite, generic set of reals. ... Finally, we exhibit an infinite family of constructions whose limiting permutons interpolate between the Wiener permuton and the recursive separable permuton; each has the same intensity permuton, providing a single two-dimensional extension of the classical arcsine law for all of them.\nWe give a self-contained proof and extend the result to the permutations generated by partial sums of uniformly random signed permutations of any fixed, finite, generic set of reals. For atomless increments with mean zero and finite variance, without assuming symmetry, we show that random-walk permutations approach a random object that we call the \"Wiener permuton,\" whose expected pattern densities equal the probabilities of the corresponding permutations generated by finite random walks with centered Laplace increments. Finally, we exhibit an infinite family of constructions whose limiting permutons interpolate between the Wiener permuton and the recursive separable permuton; each has the same intensity permuton, providing a single two-dimensional extension of the classical arcsine law for all of them.\n\npermuton probability distribution random random walk signed signed permutation permutation\n\n2026-06-30\n\nTejo Madhavarapu, T. Kyle Petersen, Peter Winkler\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the diameter of Schrijver graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2112.01884)\nciteturn4academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.9 years ago; Title: On the diameter of Schrijver graphs ... In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$.\nTitle: On the diameter of Schrijver graphs\nAuthors: Agustina Victoria Ledezma, Adrián Pastine, Pablo Torres, Mario Valencia-Pabon\nDate: Fri Dec  3 12:48:33 2021\n\nFor $k \\geq 1$ and $n \\geq 2k$, the well known Kneser graph $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ has all $k$-element subsets of an $n$-element set as vertices; two such subsets are adjacent if they are disjoint. Schrijver constructed a vertex-critical subgraph $\\operatorname{SG}(n,k)$ of $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ with the same chromatic number. In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$. We obtain an exact value of the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ when $r \\in \\{1,2\\}$ or when $r \\geq k-3$. For the remained cases, when $3 \\leq r \\leq k-4$, we obtain that the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ belongs to the integer interval $[4..k-r-1]$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the diameter of Schrijver graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2112.01884v2)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; ## On the diameter of Schrijver graphs ... DOI: 10.1016/j.dam.2024.02.019 ... In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$.\n\n## On the diameter of Schrijver graphs\n\nAgustina Victoria Ledezma, Adrián Pastine, Pablo Torres, Mario Valencia-Pabon\n\nPublished: 2021-12-03 | Updated: 2022-02-11\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1016/j.dam.2024.02.019\n\nchromatic number graph kneser\n\n### Abstract\n\nFor $k \\geq 1$ and $n \\geq 2k$, the well known Kneser graph $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ has all $k$-element subsets of an $n$-element set as vertices; two such subsets are adjacent if they are disjoint. Schrijver constructed a vertex-critical subgraph $\\operatorname{SG}(n,k)$ of $\\operatorname{KG}(n,k)$ with the same chromatic number. In this paper, we compute the diameter of the graph $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ with $r \\geq 1$. We obtain an exact value of the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ when $r \\in \\{1,2\\}$ or when $r \\geq k-3$. For the remained cases, when $3 \\leq r \\leq k-4$, we obtain that the diameter of $\\operatorname{SG}(2k+r,k)$ belongs to the integer interval $[4..k-r-1]$.\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArcsine laws for random walks generated from random permutations with applications to genomics (https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08857)\nciteturn4academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.8 years ago; Date: Fri Jan 24 01:25:59 2020 ... We also give an unexpected conjecture, along with numerical evidence and a partial proof in special cases, for the result that the number of steps above the origin by step $2n$ for the uniform permutation generated walk has exactly the same discrete arcsine distribution as for the simple random walk, even though the other statistics for these walks have very different laws.\nTitle: Arcsine laws for random walks generated from random permutations with applications to genomics\nAuthors: Xiao Fang, Han Liang Gan, Susan Holmes, Haiyan Huang, Erol Peköz, Adrian Röllin, Wenpin Tang\nDate: Fri Jan 24 01:25:59 2020\n\nA classical result for the simple symmetric random walk with $2n$ steps is that the number of steps above the origin, the time of the last visit to the origin, and the time of the maximum height all have exactly the same distribution and converge when scaled to the arcsine law. Motivated by applications in genomics, we study the distributions of these statistics for t