e distributions of these statistics for the non-Markovian random walk generated from the ascents and descents of a uniform random permutation and a Mallows($q$) permutation and show that they have the same asymptotic distributions as for the simple random walk. We also give an unexpected conjecture, along with numerical evidence and a partial proof in special cases, for the result that the number of steps above the origin by step $2n$ for the uniform permutation generated walk has exactly the same discrete arcsine distribution as for the simple random walk, even though the other statistics for these walks have very different laws. We also give explicit error bounds to the limit theorems using Stein's method for the arcsine distribution, as well as functional central limit theorems and a strong embedding of the Mallows$(q)$ permutation which is of independent interest.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSpectral Turán problem of non-bipartite graphs: Forbidden books - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0195669825000186)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.5 years ago; Crawled: today; In 2007, Nikiforov [32] proved that 𝜌⁡(𝐺)≤𝜌⁡(𝑇𝑛,𝑟) for every 𝑛-vertex 𝐾𝑟+1-free graph 𝐺, with equality if and only if 𝐺≅𝑇𝑛,𝑟, which is known as the spectral Turán theorem. ... For example, see [23], [32], [43] for 𝐾𝑟+1-free graphs, [7], [15], [28], [33], [46] for spectral problems on cycles, [2] for linear forests, [5] for odd wheels, [6] for a spectral Erdős-Sós theorem, [26], [40] for planar graphs and outplanar graphs, [3], [18], [39], [45], [47] for 𝐹-minor-free graphs (𝐹=𝐾𝑟−𝐸⁡(⋃𝑙𝑖=1𝑃𝑘𝑖)), 𝐹𝑘-minor-free graphs, 𝐾𝑟-minor-free graphs, 𝐾𝑠,𝑡-minor-free graphs and general 𝐻-minor free graphs, [8], [9], [22] for intersecting graphs, [42] for a spectral extremal conjecture, [13], [20], [31] for disjoint graphs, [48] for books and theta graphs, [29] for sparse spanning graphs, and [21] for a comprehensive survey. ... In this section, we focus on the Brualdi–Hoffman–Turán Problem on books, i.e., determining the maximum spectral radius of 𝐵𝑟+1-free graphs on 𝑚 edges. ... ### A spectral condition for the existence of a pentagon in non-bipartite graphs\n## Concluding remarks\n\nIn this section, we focus on the Brualdi–Hoffman–Turán Problem on books, i.e., determining the maximum spectral radius of 𝐵𝑟+1-free graphs on 𝑚 edges. Since isolated vertices do not have an effect on the spectral radius, we here consider graphs without isolated vertices. In 2021, Zhai, Lin and Shu [49] posed the following conjecture on books.\n\nConjecture 4.1\n\nLet 𝐺 be a 𝐵𝑟+1-free graph on 𝑚 edges and 𝑚≥𝑚0⁡(𝑟) for large enough 𝑚0⁡(𝑟). Then 𝜌⁡(𝐺)≤√𝑚,with equality if and only if 𝐺 is a complete bipartite graph.\n\n## Declaration of competing interest\n\nThe authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.\n\n## Acknowledgments\n\nThe authors would like to thank the anonymous referees for their helpful comments on improving the presentation of the paper. The research of Ruifang Liu is supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Nos. 12371361 and 12171440), Distinguished Youth Foundation of Henan Province, China (No. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1-理学自己点検・評価資料集2010-本文.indd (https://www2.sci.hokudai.ac.jp/faculty/wp/wp-content/uploads/2021/01/4.kenkyuu.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.8 years ago; 6 December, Session 2, Presentation 2 ... Arcsine Laws for Permutation Generated Random Walks ... manner: given a random uniform permutation of length n, we generate a random walk by ... problem is important in genomics, some ideas of the proof and an interesting conjecture.\nare well understood; however, for sums of dependent random variables much less is known.\nIn this talk we establish a local central limit theorem for two sums of dependent random\nvariables: the number of degree d vertices in an Erd˝os–R´enyi graph, and the number of\ngerms with d neighbours in a germ-grain model. Our approach relies on Stein’s method for\ndistributional approximation.\nArcsine Laws for Permutation Generated Random Walks\nHan Liang Gan, University of Waikato (from 2020)\n5 December, Session 3, Presentation 1\nOne of the classic arcsine laws for the simple symmetric random walk is that as the length\nof the walk increases, the proportion of time the walk spends above zero converges to the\narcsine distribution. We consider a random walk generated by a permutation in the following\nmanner: given a random uniform permutation of length n, we generate a random walk by\nthe ascents and descents of the sequence. It turns out that we still have the same arcsine\nlaw for the amount of time this walk spends above zero. In this talk we will discuss why this\nproblem is important in genomics, some ideas of the proof and an interesting conjecture.\nSeventh Wellington Workshop, 2019\n1\nTitles and Abstracts\n827--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngraph-conjectures/ARXIV_OPEN_DIFFICULTY_RANKING.md at main · graph-theory-AI/graph-conjectures · GitHub (https://github.com/graph-theory-AI/graph-conjectures/blob/main/ARXIV_OPEN_DIFFICULTY_RANKING.md)\nciteturn4search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; 88  | 3.75  | 4  | prove  | partial  | `AX_planar_recoloring_girth_5_six_colors` (`2006.09269__00`)  | Conjecture 5  | A Thomassen-type method for planar graph recoloring  | Conjecture 5 (that ${R}_{6}(G)$ has diameter $O(n)$ for planar graphs of girth at least five on $n$ vertices) appears to remain open in full generality. ... 144  | 3.50  | 4  | prove  | open  | `AX_heroes_oriented_star_delta_1_m_m` (`2306.04710__01`)  | Open problem: hero status of $\\Delta(1,m,m')$ for degree-4 oriented sta…  | On heroes in digraphs with forbidden induced 